[]{#sub.angle label="sub.angle"}

In this section we define the 3D index of an ideal triangulation. A *generalized angle structure* on a combinatorial ideal tetrahedron $\Delta$ is an assignment of real numbers (called *angles*) at each edge of $\Delta$ such that the sum of the three angles around each vertex is $1$.[^1] It is easy to see that opposite edges are assigned the same angle, thus a generalized angle structure is determined by a triple $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)
\in \mathbb R^3$ that satisfies $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=1$; see Figure [1](#fig.angles){reference-type="ref" reference="fig.angle

[]{#sub.invariance label="sub.invariance"}

Physics predicts that when defined, the 3D index $I_{\mathcal T}$ depends only on the underlying 3-manifold $M$. Recall that [@HRS] prove that every hyperbolic 3-manifold $M$ that satisfies $$\label{eq.H12}
H_1(M,\mathbb Z/2)\to H_1(M,\partial M,\mathbb Z/2) \qquad \text{is the zero map}$$ (eg. a hyperbolic link complement) admits an ideal triangulation with a strict angle structure, and conversely if $M$ has an ideal triangulation with a strict angle structure, then $M$ is irreducible, atoroidal and every boundary component of $M$ is a torus [@LT].

